window.CHDATA=window.CHDATA||{};window.CHDATA[16]={"num": 16, "title": "Viral Hepatitis (HAV, HBV, HDV, HCV, HEV)", "keyPoints": "<ul>\n<li>Five unrelated viruses share the liver as their main target. <strong>HAV and HEV</strong> are <strong>enterically transmitted</strong> and cause <strong>acute, self-limiting</strong> hepatitis (except <strong>chronic HEV in the immunosuppressed</strong>). <strong>HBV, HDV and HCV</strong> are <strong>parenterally/sexually/vertically transmitted</strong> and cause <strong>chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>HBV is the only DNA virus</strong>; its <strong>cccDNA</strong> in the hepatocyte nucleus and <strong>integration</strong> into the host genome explain why HBV can be <strong>suppressed but rarely eradicated</strong>. <strong>HCV</strong>, an RNA virus with no DNA stage, can be <strong>cured</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>HDV is a defective virus</strong> that needs <strong>HBsAg</strong> from HBV; it causes the <strong>most severe</strong> form of chronic viral hepatitis.</li>\n<li>Globally, <strong>254 million</strong> people live with HBV and <strong>50 million</strong> with HCV; viral hepatitis kills about <strong>1.3 million</strong> people a year (2022 data)<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronicity of HBV depends on age at infection:</strong> about <strong>90% after perinatal infection</strong>, <strong>&lt;5% in immunocompetent adults</strong>. HCV becomes chronic in <strong>most</strong> adults.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> acute HAV = <strong>anti-HAV IgM</strong>; acute HBV = <strong>HBsAg + anti-HBc IgM</strong>; HCV = <strong>anti-HCV, confirmed by HCV RNA</strong>; HDV = <strong>anti-HDV in every HBsAg-positive person, then HDV RNA</strong>; HEV = <strong>anti-HEV IgM + HEV RNA</strong> (RNA only in the immunosuppressed).</li>\n<li><strong>Therapy:</strong> HBV = long-term <strong>entecavir or tenofovir</strong> (or finite peginterferon); HCV = <strong>8 to 12 weeks of pangenotypic DAAs</strong>, cure (SVR12) in <strong>&gt;95%</strong>; HDV = <strong>bulevirtide</strong> and/or peginterferon; chronic HEV = <strong>reduce immunosuppression ± ribavirin</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention:</strong> effective <strong>vaccines for HAV and HBV</strong> (the HBV vaccine also prevents HDV); <strong>no vaccine for HCV</strong>; an HEV vaccine is licensed only in China.</li>\n</ul>", "redFlags": "<ul>\n<li><strong>HBV is the DNA virus.</strong> HAV, HCV, HDV and HEV are RNA viruses.</li>\n<li><strong>HAV and HEV never become chronic in the immunocompetent host.</strong> But <strong>HEV can become chronic in transplant recipients</strong>: check <strong>HEV RNA</strong>, because serology is unreliable.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolated anti-HBs = vaccinated.</strong> <strong>Anti-HBc + anti-HBs = resolved infection.</strong> Anti-HBc is never induced by vaccination.</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-HBc IgM</strong> is the only marker in the <strong>window period</strong> of acute HBV.</li>\n<li><strong>HBsAg &gt;6 months = chronic HBV.</strong> Chronicity depends on <strong>age at infection</strong> (90% perinatal, &lt;5% adults).</li>\n<li><strong>HBV causes HCC even without cirrhosis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Before rituximab or chemotherapy, always test HBsAg and anti-HBc</strong>; give prophylaxis when needed. <strong>Before HCV DAAs, check HBV</strong> status.</li>\n<li><strong>Test every HBsAg-positive patient for anti-HDV.</strong> NAs do not work against HDV.</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-HCV positive ≠ active infection.</strong> Confirm with <strong>HCV RNA</strong>. Antibodies persist after cure and <strong>do not protect</strong>: reinfection is diagnosed with RNA.</li>\n<li><strong>SVR12 = cure of HCV</strong>, but <strong>cirrhotic patients keep HCC surveillance for life</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No protease inhibitors</strong> in <strong>decompensated cirrhosis</strong>. Beware <strong>amiodarone + sofosbuvir</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnant woman + HEV gt 1</strong> = risk of <strong>fulminant hepatitis</strong>. <strong>Ribavirin is teratogenic.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Newborn of an HBsAg-positive mother:</strong> vaccine + HBIG within <strong>12–24 hours</strong>; <strong>tenofovir in the mother</strong> if HBV DNA &gt;200,000 IU/mL.</li>\n</ul>", "intro": "", "sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Microbiology", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "1.1", "title": "The five hepatitis viruses at a glance", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>HAV</strong></th>\n<th><strong>HBV</strong></th>\n<th><strong>HDV</strong></th>\n<th><strong>HCV</strong></th>\n<th><strong>HEV</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Family / genus</strong></td>\n<td><em>Picornaviridae</em> / <em>Hepatovirus</em></td>\n<td><em>Hepadnaviridae</em> / <em>Orthohepadnavirus</em></td>\n<td><em>Kolmioviridae</em> / <em>Deltavirus</em></td>\n<td><em>Flaviviridae</em> / <em>Hepacivirus</em></td>\n<td><em>Hepeviridae</em> / <em>Paslahepevirus</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Genome</strong></td>\n<td>ssRNA (+)</td>\n<td><strong>Partially double-stranded circular DNA</strong> (relaxed circular, rcDNA), replicates via <strong>reverse transcription</strong></td>\n<td>Circular ssRNA (−), ~1.7 kb, the <strong>smallest</strong> known animal virus</td>\n<td>ssRNA (+)</td>\n<td>ssRNA (+)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Envelope</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Quasi-enveloped</strong> in blood, <strong>naked</strong> in stool</td>\n<td>Enveloped (<strong>HBsAg</strong>)</td>\n<td>Envelope <strong>borrowed from HBV (HBsAg)</strong></td>\n<td>Enveloped</td>\n<td><strong>Quasi-enveloped</strong> in blood, <strong>naked</strong> in stool</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Genotypes</strong></td>\n<td>1 serotype (genotypes I–III in humans)</td>\n<td><strong>A–J</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1–8</strong></td>\n<td><strong>8 genotypes</strong> (1–8), many subtypes</td>\n<td><strong>1–4</strong> in humans (gt 1–2 human only; gt 3–4 zoonotic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Main route</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fecal-oral</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blood, sexual, perinatal</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blood, sexual</strong> (needs HBV)</td>\n<td><strong>Blood</strong> (sexual, perinatal less often)</td>\n<td><strong>Fecal-oral</strong> (gt 1–2: water; gt 3–4: food/zoonotic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Incubation</strong></td>\n<td>~4 weeks (15–50 days)</td>\n<td>~2–4 months (30–180 days)</td>\n<td>As HBV</td>\n<td>~6–8 weeks (2–26 weeks)</td>\n<td>~5–6 weeks (2–10 weeks)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chronic infection</strong></td>\n<td><strong>No</strong></td>\n<td>Yes (age-dependent)</td>\n<td>Yes (superinfection)</td>\n<td>Yes (<strong>most</strong> adults)</td>\n<td>Only in <strong>immunosuppressed</strong> (gt 3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vaccine</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong> (HBV vaccine)</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong></td>\n<td>China only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Sources:<sup class=\"cite\">[2–6, 15]</sup>.</p>"}, {"id": "1.2", "title": "HBV: structure and antigens (high yield)", "html": "<ul>\n<li>The infectious <strong>Dane particle</strong> (42 nm) carries the envelope protein <strong>HBsAg</strong> and a nucleocapsid made of <strong>HBcAg</strong> that contains the DNA and the viral <strong>polymerase</strong> (with <strong>reverse transcriptase</strong> activity)<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Infected cells also release huge excess amounts of <strong>non-infectious HBsAg particles</strong> (spheres and filaments): this is why <strong>HBsAg</strong> is so easy to detect in serum<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HBeAg</strong> is a secreted protein translated from the precore/core region. It is a <strong>marker of high replication</strong>. <strong>Precore and basal core promoter mutants</strong> do not make HBeAg but can replicate actively (<strong>HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HBcAg</strong> does not circulate free in serum, but <strong>anti-HBc</strong> is the marker of <strong>any past or present contact with the virus</strong> (vaccination does not induce it)<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HBx</strong> protein is a transcriptional transactivator implicated in <strong>hepatocarcinogenesis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Replication cycle:</strong> entry through the bile acid transporter <strong>NTCP</strong> (sodium taurocholate co-transporting polypeptide) → rcDNA is repaired in the nucleus to <strong>covalently closed circular DNA (cccDNA)</strong> → transcription → pregenomic RNA is <strong>reverse-transcribed</strong> into new DNA inside the capsid. Fragments of HBV DNA also <strong>integrate</strong> into the host genome<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>cccDNA</strong> is a stable minichromosome that <strong>nucleos(t)ide analogues do not eliminate</strong>: this is the molecular reason why therapy is usually <strong>long-term</strong> and HBV is almost never \"eradicated\"<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "1.3", "title": "HDV: a satellite virus", "html": "<ul>\n<li>HDV encodes only one protein, <strong>hepatitis delta antigen (HDAg)</strong>, in two forms (small and large). Its RNA replicates using the host <strong>RNA polymerase II</strong> and has <strong>ribozyme</strong> activity<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n<li>HDV <strong>cannot assemble without HBsAg</strong>: it uses the HBV envelope, and therefore the <strong>same entry receptor (NTCP)</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>. <strong>No HBsAg, no HDV.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Prenylation</strong> of large HDAg is required for virion assembly (target of the investigational drug <strong>lonafarnib</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "1.4", "title": "HCV: why no vaccine and why cure is possible", "html": "<ul>\n<li>The HCV RNA polymerase (<strong>NS5B</strong>) lacks proofreading → very high mutation rate and <strong>quasispecies</strong> in each patient, with <strong>hypervariable envelope regions</strong> that escape neutralizing antibodies. This explains <strong>frequent chronicity</strong>, the <strong>lack of a vaccine</strong>, and the possibility of <strong>reinfection</strong> after cure<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 15]</sup>.</li>\n<li>HCV replicates entirely in the <strong>cytoplasm</strong>, with <strong>no DNA intermediate and no integration</strong>. Once replication is blocked long enough, the virus is <strong>eliminated</strong>: <strong>SVR = cure</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Drug targets</strong> of the direct-acting antivirals (DAAs): <strong>NS3/4A protease</strong> (\"-previr\"), <strong>NS5A</strong> replication complex (\"-asvir\"), <strong>NS5B polymerase</strong> (\"-buvir\")<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "1.5", "title": "HEV", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Four genotypes infect humans. <strong>Genotypes 1 and 2</strong> infect <strong>only humans</strong> and cause <strong>waterborne epidemics</strong> in low-income countries. <strong>Genotypes 3 and 4</strong> are <strong>zoonotic</strong> (reservoir: <strong>pigs, wild boar, deer</strong>) and cause <strong>sporadic autochthonous</strong> hepatitis in high-income countries, including Europe<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><em>Rocahepevirus ratti</em> (<strong>rat HEV</strong>) has also been recognized as a rare cause of human hepatitis.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "2", "title": "Epidemiology", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "2.1", "title": "Global burden", "html": "<ul>\n<li>In 2022, <strong>254 million</strong> people were living with chronic HBV and <strong>50 million</strong> with chronic HCV; there were about <strong>1.2 million</strong> new HBV and nearly <strong>1 million</strong> new HCV infections<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Viral hepatitis caused about <strong>1.3 million deaths</strong> in 2022 (up from 1.1 million in 2019), <strong>83% due to HBV and 17% due to HCV</strong>, mostly through <strong>cirrhosis and HCC</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li>About <strong>4.5% of HBsAg-positive people</strong> (roughly <strong>12 million</strong> people) have been infected with HDV; prevalence is much higher in hepatology clinics, people who inject drugs, and in Mongolia, the Republic of Moldova and West/Central Africa<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>WHO</strong> goal is the <strong>elimination of viral hepatitis as a public health threat by 2030</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2.2", "title": "Transmission by virus", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Virus</th>\n<th>Main routes</th>\n<th>Groups at higher risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HAV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fecal-oral</strong>: person-to-person, contaminated <strong>food</strong> (e.g. shellfish, frozen berries) and <strong>water</strong></td>\n<td>Travelers to endemic areas, <strong>men who have sex with men (MSM)</strong>, people who use drugs, people experiencing homelessness, household/sexual contacts<sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HBV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Perinatal</strong> (mother to child), <strong>sexual</strong>, <strong>parenteral</strong> (shared needles, unsafe injections, transfusion where blood is not screened), <strong>horizontal</strong> in early childhood</td>\n<td>Infants of HBsAg-positive mothers, people from endemic areas, PWID, MSM, sex partners and household contacts, people on hemodialysis, people with HIV or HCV<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 8]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HDV</strong></td>\n<td>As HBV; <strong>co-infection</strong> (HBV and HDV together) or <strong>superinfection</strong> of a person with chronic HBV</td>\n<td>PWID, people from endemic regions, people on hemodialysis, sex workers<sup class=\"cite\">[3, 12]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HCV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Parenteral</strong>: <strong>injecting drug use</strong> (main route today), past <strong>transfusion</strong> (before donor screening), unsafe medical procedures, tattooing with unsterile equipment; <strong>sexual</strong> (mainly MSM with HIV) and <strong>perinatal</strong> (about 5%) less often</td>\n<td>PWID, people who received blood before the early 1990s, people in prison, people with HIV, hemodialysis<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5, 9]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HEV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Gt 1–2:</strong> fecal-contaminated <strong>drinking water</strong> (epidemics after floods/monsoons). <strong>Gt 3–4:</strong> <strong>undercooked pork, wild boar, deer meat, pork liver sausages</strong>, shellfish; also <strong>blood transfusion</strong> and organ transplantation</td>\n<td>Gt 1–2: <strong>pregnant women</strong> (severe disease). Gt 3: <strong>older men</strong>, people with <strong>chronic liver disease</strong>, <strong>transplant recipients</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6, 14]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "2.3", "title": "HBV: age at infection determines chronicity", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Age at infection</th>\n<th>Risk of chronic infection</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Perinatal</strong> (infant of HBeAg-positive mother)</td>\n<td><strong>~90%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Early childhood (1–5 years)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>~25–50%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Immunocompetent adult</strong></td>\n<td><strong>&lt;5%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Sources:<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 15]</sup>. The younger the host, the weaker the immune response and the more likely persistence. This is why <strong>birth-dose vaccination</strong> is the cornerstone of HBV control (section 8).</p>"}, {"id": "2.4", "title": "HEV in Europe", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Locally acquired <strong>HEV genotype 3</strong> is now the <strong>most common cause of acute viral hepatitis in many European countries</strong>; most infections are <strong>asymptomatic</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "3", "title": "Pathogenesis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "General principle: the immune response causes the damage", "html": "<ul>\n<li>HAV, HBV and HCV are <strong>not directly cytopathic</strong> (or only weakly so). Liver injury is mainly caused by the <strong>host immune response</strong>, especially <strong>cytotoxic CD8+ T cells</strong> killing infected hepatocytes<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Consequence: a <strong>vigorous</strong> immune response causes <strong>more severe acute hepatitis</strong> but <strong>clears</strong> the virus; a <strong>weak or tolerant</strong> response causes <strong>mild disease</strong> but <strong>persistence</strong> (e.g. perinatal HBV)<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fulminant hepatitis (acute liver failure)</strong> reflects massive immune-mediated hepatocyte death.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "HBV: natural history and phases", "html": "<p>The EASL 2025 guidelines keep the <strong>four-phase model</strong>, which describes a <strong>dynamic</strong> process; not every patient fits neatly into one phase<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase (EASL)</th>\n<th>Former name</th>\n<th>HBeAg</th>\n<th>HBV DNA</th>\n<th>ALT</th>\n<th>Liver disease</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1. HBeAg-positive chronic infection</strong></td>\n<td>\"Immune tolerant\"</td>\n<td>+</td>\n<td><strong>Very high</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td>None or minimal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2. HBeAg-positive chronic hepatitis</strong></td>\n<td>\"Immune reactive\"</td>\n<td>+</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td><strong>Elevated</strong></td>\n<td>Moderate/severe, accelerated fibrosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3. HBeAg-negative chronic infection</strong></td>\n<td>\"Inactive carrier\"</td>\n<td>− (anti-HBe +)</td>\n<td><strong>Low</strong> (usually <strong>&lt;2,000 IU/mL</strong>), HBsAg usually &lt;1,000 IU/mL</td>\n<td><strong>Normal</strong></td>\n<td>None or minimal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4. HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>− (anti-HBe +)</td>\n<td>Fluctuating, usually <strong>&gt;2,000 IU/mL</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Elevated</strong> (often fluctuating)</td>\n<td>Moderate/severe</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>EASL 2025 recommends <strong>avoiding the terms \"grey zone\" or \"intermediate phase\"</strong> for patients who do not fit a phase<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HBsAg loss</strong> (\"<strong>functional cure</strong>\") can occur spontaneously, but rarely<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HBV causes HCC even without cirrhosis</strong>, through <strong>DNA integration</strong> and <strong>HBx</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2, 15]</sup>. Risk rises with <strong>age (&gt;30–35 years)</strong>, <strong>male sex</strong>, high <strong>HBV DNA</strong>, <strong>cirrhosis</strong>, <strong>family history of HCC</strong>, HDV/HCV/HIV co-infection, alcohol and metabolic disease<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Occult HBV infection</strong>: HBV DNA in the liver (± low levels in serum) with <strong>HBsAg negative</strong>, usually <strong>anti-HBc positive</strong>. It can <strong>reactivate</strong> under immunosuppression<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "HBV reactivation", "html": "<ul>\n<li>In a person with chronic or <strong>past</strong> HBV (HBsAg-negative/anti-HBc-positive), <strong>immunosuppression</strong> can restart viral replication; when immunity recovers, a <strong>hepatitis flare</strong> follows, which can be <strong>fatal</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Highest-risk drugs:</strong> <strong>anti-CD20 antibodies (rituximab, obinutuzumab)</strong>, <strong>hematopoietic stem cell transplantation</strong>, high-dose corticosteroids in HBsAg-positive patients, and some chemotherapy regimens<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HCV DAA therapy</strong> can also trigger HBV reactivation in <strong>HBV/HCV co-infected</strong> patients, as HCV suppression removes viral interference<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.4", "title": "HDV: co-infection vs superinfection", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Co-infection</strong> (HBV + HDV at the same time)</th>\n<th><strong>Superinfection</strong> (HDV in chronic HBV carrier)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clinical picture</td>\n<td>Acute hepatitis, sometimes biphasic</td>\n<td><strong>Severe acute flare</strong> of a known HBV infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chronicity</td>\n<td><strong>Rare</strong> (&lt;5%), follows HBV clearance</td>\n<td><strong>Most cases (&gt;80%) become chronic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outcome</td>\n<td>Usually resolves; higher risk of fulminant hepatitis than HBV alone</td>\n<td><strong>Fast progression to cirrhosis</strong>, decompensation and HCC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Sources:<sup class=\"cite\">[3, 15]</sup>. Chronic hepatitis D is considered <strong>the most aggressive form of chronic viral hepatitis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</p>"}, {"id": "3.5", "title": "HCV", "html": "<ul>\n<li>About <strong>one quarter</strong> to one third of acutely infected adults <strong>clear</strong> the virus spontaneously; the rest develop <strong>chronic infection</strong> (HCV RNA persisting <strong>&gt;6 months</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 15]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Chronic hepatitis C progresses slowly: <strong>cirrhosis</strong> develops in roughly <strong>15–30% over 20–30 years</strong>. Progression is faster with <strong>alcohol</strong>, <strong>male sex</strong>, <strong>older age at infection</strong>, <strong>HIV or HBV co-infection</strong>, <strong>obesity/steatosis and diabetes</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 15]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Once cirrhosis is established, the risk of <strong>HCC</strong> is about <strong>1–4% per year</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 15]</sup>.</li>\n<li>HCV is a <strong>systemic</strong> infection: chronic B-cell stimulation causes <strong>mixed cryoglobulinemia</strong> and <strong>B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma</strong> (section 4.3)<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 15]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3.6", "title": "HEV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Gt 1–2 in pregnancy:</strong> acute liver failure with <strong>high maternal and fetal mortality</strong>, particularly in the <strong>third trimester</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6, 14]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gt 3 in immunosuppressed</strong> hosts (solid organ transplant recipients, hematologic malignancy, advanced HIV): failure to clear the virus → <strong>chronic hepatitis E</strong> (HEV RNA persisting <strong>&gt;3 months</strong>), which can progress rapidly to <strong>cirrhosis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li>HEV is also linked to <strong>neurological</strong> syndromes (<strong>Guillain-Barré syndrome</strong>, <strong>neuralgic amyotrophy</strong>) and <strong>glomerulonephritis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "4", "title": "Signs and Symptoms", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "4.1", "title": "Acute viral hepatitis (all five viruses)", "html": "<p>The clinical picture is <strong>similar for all hepatitis viruses</strong>: the virus cannot be identified at the bedside<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prodrome</strong> (days to 1–2 weeks)</td>\n<td><strong>Malaise, fatigue, anorexia, nausea, vomiting</strong>, right upper quadrant discomfort, low-grade fever; <strong>aversion to smoking</strong>; in HBV, a <strong>serum sickness-like</strong> syndrome (<strong>arthralgia/arthritis, urticarial rash</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Icteric phase</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jaundice, dark urine (choluria), pale stools</strong>, pruritus; <strong>tender hepatomegaly</strong>; prodromal symptoms usually improve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Convalescence</strong></td>\n<td>Gradual resolution over weeks; fatigue may persist</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Laboratory:</strong> <strong>aminotransferases (ALT &gt; AST)</strong> often <strong>&gt;10 times the upper limit of normal</strong> (often &gt;1,000 U/L); conjugated hyperbilirubinemia<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Most acute infections are anicteric or asymptomatic</strong>, especially in <strong>young children with HAV</strong> (most children &lt;6 years have no symptoms, while <strong>most adults</strong> are <strong>jaundiced</strong>) and in <strong>HCV</strong> (acute HCV is symptomatic in only a minority)<sup class=\"cite\">[10, 15]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Warning signs: acute liver failure", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Encephalopathy</strong> (confusion, asterixis) and <strong>coagulopathy (INR ≥1.5)</strong> in a patient without previous liver disease = <strong>acute liver failure</strong> → <strong>urgent referral to a liver transplant center</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Higher risk: <strong>HAV in older adults or with chronic liver disease</strong>, <strong>HBV (especially with HDV)</strong>, <strong>HEV gt 1 in pregnancy</strong>, <strong>HEV on top of chronic liver disease</strong> (\"acute-on-chronic\" liver failure)<sup class=\"cite\">[3, 6, 10, 15]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.3", "title": "Particular features by virus", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Virus</th>\n<th>Particular features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HAV</strong></td>\n<td>Atypical courses: <strong>relapsing</strong> hepatitis, <strong>prolonged cholestatic</strong> hepatitis; <strong>never chronic</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[10, 15]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HBV</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic infection is often <strong>silent for decades</strong> until cirrhosis or HCC. Extrahepatic: <strong>polyarteritis nodosa</strong>, <strong>membranous/membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis</strong>, serum sickness-like syndrome<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 15]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HDV</strong></td>\n<td>Severe acute hepatitis or a <strong>sudden flare</strong> in a known HBV carrier; <strong>early cirrhosis</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HCV</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic infection usually asymptomatic or with <strong>fatigue</strong> only. Extrahepatic: <strong>mixed cryoglobulinemia</strong> (purpura, arthralgia, weakness, <strong>membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis</strong>, neuropathy), <strong>B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma</strong>, <strong>porphyria cutanea tarda</strong>, <strong>lichen planus</strong>, <strong>insulin resistance/type 2 diabetes</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5, 15]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HEV</strong></td>\n<td>Mostly asymptomatic; symptomatic disease typical of <strong>older men</strong> (gt 3). <strong>Neurological</strong> (Guillain-Barré, neuralgic amyotrophy) and <strong>renal</strong> manifestations; <strong>chronic</strong> hepatitis in transplant recipients is usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong> with mildly raised ALT<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "4.4", "title": "Chronic hepatitis and its end stage", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Most patients with chronic HBV, HCV or HDV are <strong>asymptomatic</strong> until they develop <strong>cirrhosis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Decompensation:</strong> <strong>ascites</strong>, <strong>variceal bleeding</strong>, <strong>hepatic encephalopathy</strong>, <strong>jaundice</strong>; or <strong>HCC</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2–4]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "5", "title": "Diagnosis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "5.1", "title": "When to suspect viral hepatitis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Acute rise in <strong>aminotransferases</strong> (often &gt;10× ULN) with or without jaundice.</li>\n<li>Unexplained <strong>chronically raised ALT</strong>, <strong>cirrhosis</strong>, <strong>HCC</strong>, or typical <strong>extrahepatic manifestations</strong> (cryoglobulinemia, PAN, glomerulonephritis).</li>\n<li><strong>Screening</strong> of at-risk groups and, increasingly, of <strong>all adults</strong> (section 5.7).</li>\n<li>Always test for viral hepatitis <strong>before immunosuppressive therapy</strong> (HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HBs) and <strong>before DAA therapy for HCV</strong> (HBsAg, anti-HBc)<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Differential diagnosis:</strong> <strong>drug-induced liver injury</strong> (including herbal products and paracetamol), <strong>alcohol</strong>, <strong>ischemic hepatitis</strong>, <strong>autoimmune hepatitis</strong>, <strong>Wilson disease</strong>, <strong>biliary obstruction</strong>, other viruses (<strong>EBV, CMV, HSV, VZV</strong>), Q fever, leptospirosis.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.2", "title": "First-line tests for acute hepatitis of unknown cause", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Detects</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Anti-HAV IgM</strong></td>\n<td>Acute hepatitis A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HBsAg + anti-HBc IgM</strong></td>\n<td>Acute hepatitis B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Anti-HCV + HCV RNA</strong></td>\n<td>Hepatitis C (anti-HCV may still be negative early in acute infection, so test <strong>HCV RNA</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Anti-HEV IgM (± HEV RNA)</strong></td>\n<td>Acute hepatitis E</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Anti-HDV</strong></td>\n<td>In every HBsAg-positive patient</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Sources:<sup class=\"cite\">[3–6, 10]</sup>. <strong>Anti-HEV</strong> testing should be part of the routine work-up of acute hepatitis in Europe, regardless of travel history<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</p>"}, {"id": "5.3", "title": "HBV serology: interpretation (must-know)", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>HBsAg</th>\n<th>Anti-HBc (total)</th>\n<th>Anti-HBc IgM</th>\n<th>Anti-HBs</th>\n<th>Interpretation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td><strong>Susceptible</strong> (never infected, not vaccinated) → vaccinate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Immune through vaccination</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−</td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Resolved infection</strong> (natural immunity); risk of <strong>reactivation</strong> under strong immunosuppression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td><strong>Acute HBV infection</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td><strong>Chronic HBV infection</strong> (HBsAg <strong>&gt;6 months</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>−</td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td><strong>\"Isolated anti-HBc\"</strong>: past infection with waning anti-HBs (most often), <strong>occult HBV</strong>, window period of acute infection, or false positive → check <strong>HBV DNA</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Sources:<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 8]</sup>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Window period</strong> of acute HBV: HBsAg has disappeared and anti-HBs is not yet detectable; <strong>anti-HBc IgM</strong> is the only marker<sup class=\"cite\">[8, 15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-HBs ≥10 mIU/mL</strong> is considered <strong>protective</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[8, 11]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>HBeAg/anti-HBe</strong> status, <strong>HBV DNA</strong> (IU/mL), <strong>quantitative HBsAg</strong> and <strong>ALT</strong> together define the <strong>phase</strong> of chronic infection (section 3.2)<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Every HBsAg-positive person also needs: <strong>anti-HDV</strong>, <strong>anti-HCV</strong>, <strong>HIV</strong> test, <strong>anti-HAV</strong> (vaccinate if negative), and <strong>fibrosis staging</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2, 3]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.4", "title": "HCV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Step 1 – anti-HCV</strong> (screening). <strong>Step 2 – HCV RNA</strong> (or <strong>HCV core antigen</strong> where RNA is not available) to confirm <strong>current (viremic) infection</strong>; ideally as a <strong>reflex test</strong> on the same sample<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5, 9]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-HCV positive, HCV RNA negative</strong> = <strong>resolved infection</strong> (spontaneous or after treatment) or false-positive antibody. <strong>Anti-HCV stays positive for life</strong> and <strong>does not protect</strong> against reinfection<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reinfection</strong> after cure is diagnosed with <strong>HCV RNA</strong>, not antibody<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li>In <strong>immunosuppressed</strong> patients (e.g. hemodialysis, advanced HIV) and in <strong>suspected acute infection</strong>, antibodies may be absent: test <strong>HCV RNA</strong> directly<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Infants</strong> born to HCV-positive mothers: maternal antibodies cross the placenta, so test <strong>HCV RNA at 2–6 months</strong> of age<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Genotyping</strong> is no longer required before treatment with <strong>pangenotypic</strong> regimens in most patients<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.5", "title": "HDV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Test every HBsAg-positive person once for anti-HDV</strong> (EASL 2023: universal screening)<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n<li>If anti-HDV is positive → <strong>HDV RNA</strong> (quantitative PCR) to confirm <strong>active</strong> infection and to monitor therapy<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.6", "title": "HEV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immunocompetent:</strong> <strong>anti-HEV IgM</strong> (ideally with <strong>HEV RNA</strong> in blood or stool, because serological assays vary in performance)<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunosuppressed:</strong> serology is <strong>unreliable</strong> → <strong>HEV RNA by NAT</strong> in blood and/or stool is <strong>required</strong> for diagnosis<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic HEV</strong> = HEV RNA persisting <strong>&gt;3 months</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.7", "title": "Staging liver disease and screening", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Fibrosis staging</strong> with <strong>non-invasive tests</strong>: <strong>transient elastography (liver stiffness, VCTE)</strong> and serum scores (<strong>APRI, FIB-4</strong>); <strong>liver biopsy</strong> is now reserved for uncertain cases<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 4]</sup>. EASL 2025 uses liver stiffness <strong>&gt;7 kPa</strong> for significant fibrosis, <strong>&gt;8 kPa</strong> for advanced fibrosis and <strong>&gt;11 kPa</strong> for cirrhosis in HBV<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Screening recommendations (examples):</strong></li>\n<li><strong>CDC (US):</strong> HBV screening (HBsAg, anti-HBs, total anti-HBc) <strong>at least once for all adults ≥18 years</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup>; HCV screening <strong>at least once for all adults ≥18 years</strong> and <strong>during every pregnancy</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> HBsAg screening in <strong>every pregnancy</strong> to prevent mother-to-child transmission<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 7, 8]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "6", "title": "Therapy", "html": "<p><em>Principles only; no dosing.</em></p>", "subs": [{"id": "6.1", "title": "Acute hepatitis (all viruses)", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Supportive care</strong>: rest as tolerated, hydration, <strong>avoid alcohol and hepatotoxic drugs</strong> (caution with paracetamol), monitor <strong>INR, bilirubin and mental status</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Acute liver failure</strong> → <strong>transplant center</strong>. In <strong>severe acute HBV</strong> (coagulopathy, prolonged jaundice, liver failure), an <strong>NA (entecavir or tenofovir)</strong> is indicated<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Acute HCV:</strong> no need to wait for spontaneous clearance; EASL and AASLD-IDSA recommend treating <strong>recently acquired HCV</strong> with the <strong>same DAA regimens</strong> as chronic infection, also to prevent onward transmission<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.2", "title": "Chronic hepatitis B", "html": "<p><strong>Goals<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>:</strong> prevent progression to <strong>cirrhosis, decompensation and HCC</strong>, prevent transmission and improve survival. The <strong>ideal endpoint</strong> is <strong>HBsAg loss (functional cure)</strong>: HBsAg loss confirmed twice, <strong>6 months apart</strong>, with <strong>HBV DNA undetectable</strong>. More often, the realistic endpoint is <strong>sustained HBV DNA suppression</strong> on therapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Main treatment indications (EASL 2025)<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Treat?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chronic hepatitis B</strong> (HBeAg+ or −): <strong>HBV DNA &gt;2,000 IU/mL</strong> and <strong>ALT &gt;ULN</strong> and/or at least <strong>significant fibrosis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cirrhosis</strong> (compensated or decompensated) with <strong>any detectable HBV DNA</strong>, regardless of ALT</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HBeAg-positive chronic infection</strong> with <strong>extrahepatic manifestations</strong>, <strong>increased HCC risk</strong> (e.g. family history), <strong>immunosuppression</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pregnant women</strong> with <strong>HBV DNA &gt;200,000 IU/mL</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong> (to prevent mother-to-child transmission, section 8.2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HBeAg-positive</strong>, age <strong>&lt;30 years</strong>, persistently normal ALT, no fibrosis, no family history of HCC, no immunosuppression</td>\n<td>Immediate treatment <strong>not supported</strong> by current evidence → monitor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>EASL considers ALT <strong>&gt;40 U/L</strong> as generally elevated, but recommends sex-specific laboratory ULNs where available; <strong>normal ALT alone does not exclude treatment</strong>. In principle, <strong>every HBsAg-positive person with detectable HBV DNA is a candidate</strong> for therapy<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>WHO 2024</strong> guidelines <strong>simplified and expanded</strong> the treatment criteria to make treatment accessible in low-resource settings<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Drug options<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Drugs</th>\n<th>Features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nucleos(t)ide analogues (NAs)</strong>, long-term</td>\n<td><strong>Entecavir (ETV)</strong>, <strong>tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)</strong>, <strong>tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)</strong></td>\n<td>Oral, very well tolerated, <strong>high barrier to resistance</strong>; suppress HBV DNA in almost all patients; <strong>HBsAg loss is rare</strong>, so therapy is usually <strong>indefinite</strong>. TDF can affect <strong>kidney and bone</strong>; TAF has less renal/bone toxicity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pegylated interferon-α (PegIFN)</strong>, finite (about 48 weeks)</td>\n<td>PegIFN-α</td>\n<td>Immunomodulatory, <strong>finite</strong> course, <strong>higher chance of HBsAg loss</strong> in selected patients, but many <strong>side effects</strong>; <strong>contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis</strong> and pregnancy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lamivudine, adefovir and telbivudine</strong> are <strong>no longer recommended</strong> as first-line drugs because of their <strong>low barrier to resistance</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stopping NAs:</strong> safe after <strong>confirmed HBsAg loss</strong>. Stopping in HBeAg-negative non-cirrhotic patients without HBsAg loss is possible only in <strong>selected</strong> patients with <strong>close monitoring</strong>, because <strong>relapses (sometimes severe)</strong> are common. <strong>Never stop NAs in cirrhosis</strong> without HBsAg loss<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Prophylaxis of reactivation:</strong> HBsAg-positive patients starting immunosuppression need <strong>NA prophylaxis</strong> (ETV or tenofovir). <strong>HBsAg-negative/anti-HBc-positive</strong> patients need <strong>prophylaxis if high risk</strong> (e.g. <strong>anti-CD20 antibodies, stem cell transplantation</strong>) or otherwise <strong>monitoring</strong> (HBsAg/HBV DNA)<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.3", "title": "Chronic hepatitis D", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Bulevirtide</strong>, a lipopeptide that <strong>blocks the NTCP receptor</strong> and so prevents HBV/HDV entry into hepatocytes, is approved in the <strong>EU</strong> for compensated chronic hepatitis D. In the phase 3 MYR301 trial it achieved a combined virological and biochemical response in about <strong>45–48%</strong> of patients at week 48, compared with 2% without treatment<sup class=\"cite\">[3, 13]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>PegIFN-α</strong> (off-label) achieves sustained HDV RNA suppression in only a <strong>minority</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>NAs are ineffective against HDV</strong> (HDV does not use the HBV polymerase); they are added only if HBV DNA replication itself requires treatment<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Advanced disease → <strong>liver transplantation</strong> (with lifelong HBV prophylaxis)<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.4", "title": "Chronic hepatitis C", "html": "<p><strong>Who:</strong> <strong>all patients with HCV RNA</strong>, including recently acquired infection, without delay (\"<strong>treat all</strong>\"). The aim is <strong>cure</strong>, defined as <strong>sustained virological response (SVR12)</strong>: <strong>HCV RNA undetectable 12 weeks after the end of treatment</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Regimen (pangenotypic)</th>\n<th>Targets</th>\n<th>Usual duration</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir</strong></td>\n<td>NS5B + NS5A</td>\n<td><strong>12 weeks</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Glecaprevir/pibrentasvir</strong></td>\n<td>NS3/4A + NS5A</td>\n<td><strong>8 weeks</strong> (treatment-naïve, including compensated cirrhosis)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir/voxilaprevir</strong></td>\n<td>NS5B + NS5A + NS3/4A</td>\n<td><strong>Retreatment</strong> after DAA failure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Sources:<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SVR rates &gt;95%</strong>, oral, short, well tolerated. <strong>Interferon is no longer used</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li>DAAs are <strong>always combined</strong> (two or three drugs acting on different viral targets) and <strong>never used as monotherapy</strong>: the high mutation rate of HCV would rapidly select <strong>resistant variants</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Simplified treatment</strong> (AASLD-IDSA): treatment-naïve adults <strong>without cirrhosis</strong> (or with compensated cirrhosis) can be treated <strong>without genotyping</strong> and with <strong>minimal on-treatment monitoring</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Before treatment:</strong> check <strong>HBsAg</strong> and <strong>anti-HBc</strong> (HBV reactivation risk), <strong>drug-drug interactions</strong> (e.g. <strong>protease inhibitors are contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis</strong>; <strong>amiodarone</strong> + sofosbuvir can cause severe <strong>bradycardia</strong>; some anticonvulsants and rifampicin reduce DAA levels), and <strong>fibrosis stage</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Decompensated cirrhosis:</strong> <strong>no NS3/4A protease inhibitors</strong>; use sofosbuvir/velpatasvir (± ribavirin) and evaluate for <strong>liver transplantation</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Treat <strong>people who inject drugs</strong> and people on opioid agonist therapy: <strong>active drug use is not a contraindication</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.5", "title": "Hepatitis E", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute HEV</strong> in immunocompetent patients: <strong>supportive care</strong>, no antiviral therapy<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic HEV in transplant recipients:</strong> first <strong>reduce immunosuppression</strong> if possible (clears HEV in about one third); if HEV RNA persists, <strong>ribavirin for 3 months</strong> (longer if needed)<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ribavirin</strong> is <strong>teratogenic</strong> and <strong>contraindicated in pregnancy</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.6", "title": "Hepatitis A", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Supportive care only</strong>; there is no specific antiviral<sup class=\"cite\">[10, 15]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "7", "title": "Follow-up", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "7.1", "title": "Acute hepatitis", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Monitor <strong>aminotransferases, bilirubin and INR</strong> until normalization<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HBV:</strong> test <strong>HBsAg</strong> at 6 months. <strong>Persistence &gt;6 months = chronic infection</strong>; resolution = <strong>HBsAg loss with anti-HBs seroconversion</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2, 8]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HCV:</strong> if untreated, <strong>HCV RNA persisting &gt;6 months</strong> = chronic infection; but treatment is recommended without waiting (section 6.1)<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.2", "title": "Chronic hepatitis B", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Untreated</strong> patients: lifelong monitoring of <strong>ALT, HBV DNA</strong> and <strong>quantitative HBsAg</strong> (e.g. every 6–12 months, depending on phase), because the <strong>phase can change</strong> (e.g. HBeAg-negative infection → HBeAg-negative hepatitis)<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>On NAs:</strong> <strong>HBV DNA and ALT</strong> every 3–6 months at first, then every 6–12 months; <strong>renal function</strong> (and phosphate) on tenofovir; check <strong>adherence</strong>; <strong>HBsAg</strong> yearly to detect functional cure<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HCC surveillance</strong> with <strong>ultrasound every 6 months</strong> (± alpha-fetoprotein) in <strong>all patients with cirrhosis</strong> and in <strong>non-cirrhotic patients at increased HCC risk</strong> (age, sex, family history, ethnic origin, risk scores). <strong>Antiviral therapy reduces but does not abolish</strong> HCC risk<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cirrhosis:</strong> also screen for <strong>esophageal varices</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.3", "title": "Chronic hepatitis C after treatment", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Confirm <strong>SVR12</strong> (HCV RNA 12 weeks after the end of therapy)<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Without advanced fibrosis or cirrhosis:</strong> patients <strong>can be discharged</strong>; ALT normal and SVR = cure<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>With advanced fibrosis (F3) or cirrhosis (F4):</strong> <strong>continue HCC surveillance every 6 months</strong> (ultrasound ± AFP) <strong>for life</strong>, and surveillance for <strong>varices</strong>: <strong>SVR reduces but does not eliminate the HCC risk</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ongoing risk</strong> (PWID, MSM with high-risk sex): retest with <strong>HCV RNA</strong> at least <strong>yearly</strong>, because <strong>reinfection</strong> is possible<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Treatment failure:</strong> retreatment with <strong>sofosbuvir/velpatasvir/voxilaprevir</strong> (or other regimens based on resistance and genotype)<sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.4", "title": "Chronic hepatitis D", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>HDV RNA</strong>, <strong>ALT</strong> and <strong>HBV DNA</strong> during treatment; <strong>HCC surveillance</strong> every 6 months; HDV progresses fast, so fibrosis must be reassessed regularly<sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7.5", "title": "Chronic hepatitis E", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>HEV RNA</strong> in blood (and stool) at the end of ribavirin therapy and <strong>3 months later</strong>, to confirm sustained virological response<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "8", "title": "Vaccines and Prophylaxis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "8.1", "title": "Hepatitis A", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Inactivated vaccine</strong>, highly immunogenic; <strong>two doses</strong> give long-lasting protection (also available combined with HBV vaccine)<sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Indications (ACIP):</strong> routine childhood vaccination (US); <strong>travelers</strong> to endemic areas; <strong>MSM</strong>; <strong>people who use drugs</strong>; people experiencing homelessness; <strong>chronic liver disease</strong> (including HBV/HCV); <strong>HIV</strong>; occupational risk; close contacts of international adoptees<sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-exposure prophylaxis</strong> (within <strong>2 weeks</strong> of exposure): <strong>HAV vaccine</strong> for healthy people <strong>≥12 months</strong>; <strong>immune globulin</strong> (± vaccine) for <strong>infants &lt;12 months</strong>, <strong>immunocompromised</strong> people, <strong>chronic liver disease</strong> and those for whom vaccine is contraindicated<sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hygiene</strong>: safe water, hand washing, food safety.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.2", "title": "Hepatitis B (and therefore D)", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Recombinant HBsAg vaccine</strong>, given in <strong>3 doses</strong> (0, 1, 6 months; other schedules exist). Protection is defined as <strong>anti-HBs ≥10 mIU/mL</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[8, 11]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>WHO:</strong> universal infant vaccination with a <strong>birth dose within 24 hours</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Italy:</strong> HBV vaccination has been <strong>mandatory for all newborns since 1991</strong> (Law 165/1991)<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>ACIP (2022):</strong> universal vaccination of <strong>all adults aged 19–59 years</strong>, and of adults ≥60 years with risk factors<sup class=\"cite\">[11]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-vaccination anti-HBs testing</strong> for people at ongoing risk (healthcare workers, hemodialysis, HIV, sex partners of HBsAg-positive people, infants of HBsAg-positive mothers)<sup class=\"cite\">[8, 11]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Because HDV needs HBV, the <strong>HBV vaccine also prevents HDV</strong>. There is <strong>no protection</strong> against HDV superinfection for people who are <strong>already HBsAg-positive</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Prevention of mother-to-child transmission<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 7]</sup>:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Screen</strong> every pregnant woman for <strong>HBsAg</strong>.</li>\n<li>If <strong>HBV DNA &gt;200,000 IU/mL</strong> (or HBeAg-positive where DNA is not available, per WHO), give <strong>tenofovir (TDF)</strong> from the <strong>second/third trimester</strong> (around weeks <strong>24–28</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 7]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Newborn:</strong> <strong>HBV vaccine birth dose plus hepatitis B immune globulin (HBIG)</strong> within <strong>12–24 hours of birth</strong>, then complete the vaccine series; test the infant for <strong>HBsAg and anti-HBs</strong> after the series<sup class=\"cite\">[2, 8]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Breastfeeding is not contraindicated</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Post-exposure prophylaxis</strong> (e.g. needlestick): depends on the source's HBsAg status and the exposed person's vaccination/anti-HBs status; options are <strong>vaccination</strong> and <strong>HBIG</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup>.</p>"}, {"id": "8.3", "title": "Hepatitis C", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>No vaccine</strong> (section 1.4) and <strong>no post-exposure prophylaxis</strong>. After an exposure, test with <strong>HCV RNA</strong> and <strong>treat if infection occurs</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Prevention: <strong>harm reduction</strong> for PWID (<strong>needle and syringe programs</strong>, <strong>opioid agonist therapy</strong>), screening of <strong>blood and organs</strong>, <strong>infection control</strong> in healthcare, safer sex, and \"<strong>treatment as prevention</strong>\" (curing infected people stops onward transmission)<sup class=\"cite\">[1, 4, 5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.4", "title": "Hepatitis E", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Gt 3 (Europe):</strong> <strong>cook pork, wild boar and game meat thoroughly</strong>; immunosuppressed patients and people with chronic liver disease should avoid undercooked meat and shellfish<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gt 1–2:</strong> <strong>safe drinking water</strong> and sanitation<sup class=\"cite\">[14]</sup>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>recombinant HEV vaccine</strong> (HEV 239, Hecolin) is <strong>licensed only in China</strong>. WHO does <strong>not</strong> recommend routine use, but it may be considered in <strong>outbreaks</strong> and for high-risk groups such as pregnant women in outbreak settings<sup class=\"cite\">[14]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Several countries screen <strong>blood donations</strong> for HEV RNA<sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}], "open": ["Compare HAV, HBV, HDV, HCV and HEV in terms of genome, route of transmission and ability to cause chronic infection.", "Explain the role of cccDNA and integration in HBV persistence, and why HCV can be cured while HBV usually cannot.", "Describe the four phases of chronic HBV infection, with their HBeAg, HBV DNA and ALT profiles.", "Interpret the following HBV serological profiles: (a) anti-HBs positive only; (b) HBsAg, anti-HBc IgM positive; (c) anti-HBc and anti-HBs positive; (d) isolated anti-HBc.", "Distinguish HDV co-infection from superinfection, and explain how HDV infection is diagnosed.", "Describe the two-step diagnosis of HCV infection and the meaning of an anti-HCV-positive, HCV RNA-negative result.", "List the extrahepatic manifestations of HCV and HBV infection.", "What are the main indications for treating chronic hepatitis B, and what are the first-line drugs?", "Describe the general principles of DAA treatment of chronic hepatitis C: why DAAs are always combined and never used as monotherapy, the goal of treatment, and the definition of SVR.", "Describe the epidemiology of HEV genotypes 1–2 versus 3–4 and the clinical consequences in pregnant women and transplant recipients."], "refs": "<ol>\n<li>World Health Organization. <em>Global hepatitis report 2024: action for access in low- and middle-income countries.</em> Geneva: WHO; 2024. https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240091672</li>\n<li>European Association for the Study of the Liver. EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of hepatitis B virus infection. <em>J Hepatol.</em> 2025;83(2):502–583. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhep.2025.03.018</li>\n<li>European Association for the Study of the Liver. EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on hepatitis delta virus. <em>J Hepatol.</em> 2023;79(2):433–460. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhep.2023.05.001</li>\n<li>European Association for the Study of the Liver. EASL recommendations on treatment of hepatitis C: final update of the series. <em>J Hepatol.</em> 2020;73(5):1170–1218. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhep.2020.08.018</li>\n<li>Bhattacharya D, Aronsohn A, Price J, Lo Re V; AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance Panel. Hepatitis C guidance 2023 update: AASLD-IDSA recommendations for testing, managing, and treating hepatitis C virus infection. <em>Clin Infect Dis.</em> 2023. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciad319 (continuously updated at https://www.hcvguidelines.org)</li>\n<li>European Association for the Study of the Liver. EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on hepatitis E virus infection. <em>J Hepatol.</em> 2018;68(6):1256–1271. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhep.2018.03.005</li>\n<li>World Health Organization. <em>Guidelines for the prevention, diagnosis, care and treatment for people with chronic hepatitis B infection.</em> Geneva: WHO; 2024. https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240090903</li>\n<li>Conners EE, Panagiotakopoulos L, Hofmeister MG, et al. Screening and testing for hepatitis B virus infection: CDC recommendations — United States, 2023. <em>MMWR Recomm Rep.</em> 2023;72(1):1–25. https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.rr7201a1</li>\n<li>Schillie S, Wester C, Osborne M, Wesolowski L, Ryerson AB. CDC recommendations for hepatitis C screening among adults — United States, 2020. <em>MMWR Recomm Rep.</em> 2020;69(2):1–17. https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.rr6902a1</li>\n<li>Nelson NP, Weng MK, Hofmeister MG, et al. Prevention of hepatitis A virus infection in the United States: recommendations of the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices, 2020. <em>MMWR Recomm Rep.</em> 2020;69(5):1–38. https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.rr6905a1</li>\n<li>Weng MK, Doshani M, Khan MA, et al. Universal hepatitis B vaccination in adults aged 19–59 years: updated recommendations of the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices — United States, 2022. <em>MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep.</em> 2022;71(13):477–483. https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7113a1</li>\n<li>Stockdale AJ, Kreuels B, Henrion MYR, et al. The global prevalence of hepatitis D virus infection: systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>J Hepatol.</em> 2020;73(3):523–532. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhep.2020.04.008</li>\n<li>Wedemeyer H, Aleman S, Brunetto MR, et al. A phase 3, randomized trial of bulevirtide in chronic hepatitis D. <em>N Engl J Med.</em> 2023;389(1):22–32. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2213429</li>\n<li>World Health Organization. Hepatitis E vaccine: WHO position paper, May 2015. <em>Wkly Epidemiol Rec.</em> 2015;90(18):185–200. https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/WER9018</li>\n<li>Bennett JE, Dolin R, Blaser MJ, eds. <em>Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases.</em> 9th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2020. Chapters on hepatitis A, B, C, D and E viruses.</li>\n<li>Panagiotakopoulos L, Sandul AL, Conners EE, et al. CDC recommendations for hepatitis C testing among perinatally exposed infants and children — United States, 2023. <em>MMWR Recomm Rep.</em> 2023;72(4):1–21. https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.rr7204a1</li>\n<li>Repubblica Italiana. Legge 27 maggio 1991, n. 165: Obbligatorietà della vaccinazione contro l'epatite virale B. Gazzetta Ufficiale, 1991.</li>\n</ol>"};
